# 6. THE ORIGINAL PINBALL PROJECT

The original pinball game is an important part of the development history.

It represents the practical experience that helped establish the founder's interest in game engines and development architecture.

The project evolved beyond a simple pinball table.

It included experiments with:

- Multiple ball saves.
- Ball transportation.
- Multiple levels.
- Depth and Z positioning.
- Camera movement.
- Ball shadows.
- Visual scaling.
- Physics and visual ball separation.
- Targets and bumpers.
- Bonus systems.
- Rollovers.
- Animated objects.

The founder was particularly interested in creating a 2.5D environment.

The visual goal was to make the ball appear to move between different depths and levels while maintaining believable movement and weight.

An important design idea was separating the physical ball from its visual representation and shadow.

This allowed the possibility of controlling physics independently from visual depth.

The original project also included:

- Spinner.
- Pop bumpers.
- Coin/star marker.
- Three rollover lanes.
- Six round targets arranged in two banks.
- Three rectangular drop targets.
- Two bottom ball saves.
- A planned lucky-shot ball save.

This project provides valuable experience for future engine development.

